title: '2026-11-15: Libby''s reading of the machine answer is applied, and the answer is mailed to Jeff from Mario''s graph only.'
pid: 2ac8b1b7-a126-4aef-be13-b87881567de3
body: |2-
  Mario's fork of Jeff's 'to Jeff': d80a2ffe-3c77-499e-af77-fafc70e6c7e8.
  Mario's 'to Jeff' under it: 36b45aa5-e9e6-40d8-bd4e-ed788a86e3d5,
  holding the answer, 71fea003-732f-4c14-ac71-b98bc05de105. The save
  forked only Jeff's 'to Jeff', into 'mario'; nothing foreign was written.
  Tracy has no 'to Jeff' and her server was down, so it went from
  Mario's only.
  The root's final title: "If you want the work off this laptop, rent a
  Hetzner CPX32 (4 vCPU, 8 GB, about $34 a month) for a month's trial;
  no machine will make missions faster, since they wait on Claude's
  thinking, not on the tests."
  Libby's findings: all applied; none declined. Four were met another
  way than she wrote:
  - Her "To decide" line for the root became three notes, the root's
    first children (rent or stay; which machine, Hetzner CPX32 named;
    whether his own server moves, default it stays), as you asked.
  - "About 30 s" saved by four test jobs: measured instead, 47 s at one
    job on four efficiency cores, so the title says 31 s.
  - Her "8 to 8.5 GB": with every figure now in GB (she asked for one
    unit), the parts sum to about 8 to 9 GB; the note says that.
  - The list of packages stays in the Docker note, marked "for the
    install script, not for you", rather than moved behind a link.
  The times she asked for ("Claude can do it in N hours") are my
  estimates, and the notes say so: under an hour for the 42-second
  test, a few hours for copying fixtures, half a day for an install
  script. The euro rate is the ECB's of 13 November, 1.1052; a currency
  site gave 1.102 and a search snippet 1.156. That a US customer pays
  no European VAT is stated as unconfirmed.
  Jeff's 'to Jeff' file shows as modified in his Git, but by his own
  edit of 2026-11-14 14:55, not by this save.
